    "done_when": "Follow followup f-aocfio-reflex-in-flight-reads-the-record on plan an-orchestrator-cannot-forget-its-obligations. _reflex_review_in_flight in reckon/crew/obligations.py treats a review as in flight when runs.pointer_path(review_run_id).exists(). The reflex's recorded-dispatch rule in reckon/crew/recovery.py also requires read_pointer() to return a record. So a pointer file that exists but is unreadable withholds review-missing in obligations, while the reflex is free to dispatch another review. Both now call one shared predicate, defined once where the reflex's rule lives, with no second copy. A new tests/test_in_flight_review_has_one_predicate.py works over a synthesised temporary crew home, never the real ~/.config/reckon. It writes a pointer file that exists but is unreadable, and asserts that the obligations reader and the reflex give the same answer. A readable live pointer and an absent pointer are covered too. The negative control keeps the exists-only check in obligations.py and must fail the unreadable-pointer case. One base log and one head log over tests/test_in_flight_review_has_one_predicate.py tests/test_crew_obligations.py tests/test_mcp_crew_obligations.py tests/test_reflex_dispatches_one_repair.py and any test that calls _reflex_review_in_flight or the reflex's recorded-dispatch rule (grep tests/ and name them), each with first line `revision: <full sha>`, run after the last commit with a clean tree, show zero added failures by id. Use the absolute venv python and a neutral /tmp basetemp you delete afterwards, and remove any scratch trees you create.",
